WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.580
On a microscopic level,

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:03.580
the Earth’s spin rate

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.840
and axis are changing constantly

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.140
The drift of continents,

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.620
changes in ocean currents,

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.080
and the rebounding of the crust

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.240
since the retreat of ice-age glaciers,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.840
all shift mass around and

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.340
alter Earth’s spin rate and axis.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.020
Even hurricanes and earthquakes

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.540
can give the Earth a tiny nudge

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.260
this way or that.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.380
We need this exact information to launch rockets

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:25.440
Without these corrections

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.640
GPS satellites would drift into irrelevance

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.200
within weeks

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.500
Right now we rely on

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.260
“Very Long Baseline Interferometry”

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.900
to track Earth’s rotation and spin axis

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.120
But the system requires dozens of observatories

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.320
to give up valuable astronomy time.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.880
And computing the results can sometimes take days.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.040
By contrast,

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.660
the ROMY ring laser promises to make fast

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.060
and potentially continuous measurements.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:52.820
This is ROMY

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.720
being built over the course of 6 months

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.200
At the end it’s buried underground

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.200
with only a few access points sticking up.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.200
Last month, it turned on all four of its

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.320
triangular laser rings for the first time.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.080
ROMY, like other ring lasers,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.080
relies on the Sagnac effect,

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.420
in which two beams travel in

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:13.360
opposite directions around the same path

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.660
Because the Earth is rotating

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.440
in the direction of one of the lasers,

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.360
it incurs an extra path length

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.680
that the laser accommodates

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.280
by stretching
its wavelengths

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.460
and lowering its frequency.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.160
The frequency of the counter-rotating laser rises

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.100
When interfered,

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.560
the slightly clashing lasers

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.640
frequencies create beats --

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.560
the same pulsing beats that piano tuners

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.740
try to eliminate

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.560
as they strike a note and a tuning fork

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.360
at the same time.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.360
The beat frequency is a direct measure

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.860
of the rotation that causes it.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:44.260
With its 12 meter arms,

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:47.400
ROMY is more sensitive than smaller ring lasers.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.380
And instead of one square ring,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.160
it has four triangular ones.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.460
Three are required to pin down

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.120
rotations in any direction,

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.720
while the fourth adds redundancy.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:58.920
Being so new, however,

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.700
ROMY is plagued by experimental drift.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.740
The machine is settling in soft sediments,

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.020
and its steel tubes will flex with the temperature

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:07.820
swings of day and night.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.140
Scientists will try to isolate

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:11.580
and eliminate these sources of noise.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.700
But there is one type of noise that scientists

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.400
want ROMY to capture:

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.720
tilt motions from earthquakes.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.120
Traditionally, seismologists

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.860
could only measure translation,

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.540
or displacements along any of

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:24.180
the three cardinal axes.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.960
But earthquakes also contain tilt motions,

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.580
which rotate the ground without shifting it.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.060
Engineers have been designing buildings

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.040
for only one type of shaking,

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:34.460
when really they should be designed

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:36.500
for the two different inputs

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.200
Although ROMY can record these motions

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.580
from distant earthquakes, it can’t move.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.280
That’s why scientists want to build

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.720
smaller, portable rotation sensors,

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:45.980
based on fiber optics,

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:48.200
that could be sent out to measure tilt motions

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:49.600
around the world.

